  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nAs of 230AM Saturday morning, a highly amplified pattern was present \nacross the CONUS with trough axis' across the coasts and ridging \nacross the central US. A weak perturbations continue to lift north \non the eastern periphery of the western CONUS trough. This continues \nto yield scattered showers across mainly far western KS and CO. A \nfew showers cannot be ruled out across central KS this morning but \nagain the best chances today will remain across western KS. With the \nforecast area remaining under the influence of the midlevel ridge, \ntemperatures will remain above normal through the weekend with highs \nin the upper 70s to near 80. By late tonight into Sunday, a strong \nnorthern stream midlevel trough will slide across the southern \nCanadian provinces, deamplifying the ridge axis across the MS \nValley. As the ridge deamplifies, the western CONUS trough will \neventually eject into the Plains early next week. It's arrival will \nbring rain chances back to the area and temperatures near-normal. A \nsecondary shortwave trough across the Pacific NW may emerge across \nthe central Plains towards the middle of next week, bringing \nadditional rain chances to the forecast area Wednesday. Another deep \nmidlevel trough is possible across the western CONUS near the end of \nnext week into next weekend. This could support additional rain \nchances across the central Plains during that time period.",
